      "topic_id": "c8ee81ef-1264-4cfc-b9da-a780bc990834",
      "text": "Under the 2024 BP 344 IRR, what is the minimum height for edge protection curbs on accessible ramps, and what is the rationale for this requirement?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "50 mm minimum; to prevent water ingress into the ramp structure, which is not the primary concern for edge protection"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "75 mm minimum; to provide sufficient visual contrast between the ramp surface and the drop edge, enhancing safety for visually impaired users"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "100 mm minimum; to prevent wheelchair casters or crutch tips from slipping over the edge and causing falls"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "150 mm minimum; to meet the structural deflection requirements for cantilevered ramp edges, which is not typically a requirement for edge protection curbs"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "correct_value": null,
      "accepted_answers": null,
      "value_tolerance": 0,
      "question_type": "mcq",
      "rationale": "Section A.1.2.h of the 2024 IRR Appendix A requires minimum 100mm high curbs/raised edges on both sides of accessible ramps with open edges. The rationale is safety: wheelchair casters and crutch tips can slip over low edges, causing falls. This curb requirement operates alongside the 1,100mm minimum guard/railing height (per PD 1096 Rule XII) for ramps elevated more than 750mm above adjacent grade. The curb handles the lower-level fall prevention; the guardrail handles the higher-level protection.",
      "explanation": null,
      "code_citation": null,
